  "tax_note": "Montana levies a 20% state tax on adult-use sales and 4% on medical sales, and counties may add a local option tax of up to 3% (Montana Department of Revenue).",
  "summary": "Montana's adult-use sales began in January 2022 and have grown slowly but steadily since. The Department of Revenue estimates $28.1 million in sales for September 2026, of which $25.6 million was adult-use. Annual sales were about $324.3 million in 2024 and $326.9 million in 2025. Medical sales have fallen every year as patients move to adult-use stores, even though medical carries a much lower tax. Summer tourism lifts sales from June to August. Many rural counties still prohibit adult-use stores, which limits access in parts of the state.",
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much cannabis does Montana sell a month?",
      "a": "The Montana Department of Revenue estimates $28.1 million in cannabis sales for September 2026: $25.6 million adult-use and $2.5 million medical."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much cannabis has Montana sold since legalization?",
      "a": "From January 2022 through September 2026, estimated sales total about $1.52 billion, including $1.26 billion adult-use, per the Department of Revenue."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the cannabis tax rate in Montana?",
      "a": "20% on adult-use and 4% on medical purchases, plus up to 3% where counties have adopted a local option tax."
    }
  ],
